The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
// In aie2_send_mgmt_msg_wait():
ndev->mgmt_chann = NULL;

// In aie2_hw_stop():
// (likely) some operation on ndev->mgmt_chann without NULL check,
// e.g., xdna_mailbox_stop_channel(ndev->mgmt_chann);
```
Reason: `aie2_send_mgmt_msg_wait()` sets `ndev->mgmt_chann` to `NULL` when `-ETIME` is returned. Later, `aie2_hw_stop()` accesses `ndev->mgmt_chann` without checking for `NULL`, causing a NULL pointer dereference.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
// Added helper function aie2_destroy_mgmt_chann():
void aie2_destroy_mgmt_chann(struct amdxdna_dev_hdl *ndev)
{
    struct amdxdna_dev *xdna = ndev->xdna;
    drm_WARN_ON(&xdna->ddev, !mutex_is_locked(&xdna->dev_lock));
    if (!ndev->mgmt_chann)
        return;
    xdna_mailbox_stop_channel(ndev->mgmt_chann);
    xdna_mailbox_destroy_channel(ndev->mgmt_chann);
    ndev->mgmt_chann = NULL;
}

// Changed call site in aie2_send_mgmt_msg_wait():
if (ret == -ETIME)
    aie2_destroy_mgmt_chann(ndev);

// Additionally, NULL checks are added in aie2_hw_stop() before accessing mgmt_chann.
```
Reason: The new helper `aie2_destroy_mgmt_chann()` encapsulates the destruction logic with an explicit NULL check, preventing a double-free or use-after-free. Adding NULL checks in `aie2_hw_stop()` ensures that the channel is only accessed when not NULL, eliminating the dereference.

